{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342206,"nonce":"d032bm75iIA+Ni7WaPtvRuI8cWM=","title":"Each LLVM install keeps its own MCJIT engine, TargetMachine and subtarget for the process's life","message":"LLVMJitBackend.engines grows by one ExecutionEngine per promoted install, each with its own TargetMachine, an AArch64Subtarget from getSubtargetImpl (about 1.15 MB) and the module IR.\n\nEvidence (Mac, MallocStackLogging at _exit, frames under compile_module -\u003e MCJIT::emitObject -\u003e getSubtargetImpl): tokenize zl 21.1 MB (live LLVM 25.3, plus 29.5 MB dirty free malloc), records 12.2, pipeline 6.1, binary_trees 4.8, nbody 4.1; locals zl 228 MB live LLVM (compile_function 130). NUC mallinfo2 minus Rust heap: nextvar 63 MB llvm vs 11.6 default.\n\nSavings: 4 to 25 MB per benchmark under ZYLUA_LLVM=1, growing about 1.2 MB per install; up to 228 MB on locals.lua. Install latency should drop too (see 2708410).\n\nFix: one engine (or one shared TargetMachine) that each promoted module is added to; free each module's IR once its code is emitted. Code sections a published pointer names must stay alive (tier-up thread exit race). Package MEM-llvm-one-engine, after fix/llvm-no-reopt lands (it edits llvm_jit_backend.rs).","files":null}]}